2186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399328 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66a239a8-93d6-518d-96e1-a3a28f06e1cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98768" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399329 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4670b2c4-578c-543d-913f-4a2e02949175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399329 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399330 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3279ea5-57df-56e5-a59e-a9baf5354ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399331 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}